lesson k-what-melts v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=6 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What melts, and what changes"
  goal "Say what melts when it is warmed and sets when it is cooled, and tell a change that goes back from one that does not."

  look {
    scene 18x12 {
      beforeafter ch change="melt-chocolate" show=1 names=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Warm chocolate, ice or butter and they melt: hard turns runny. Cool them and they set hard
      again. Some changes cannot go back: a cooked egg never goes runny again, and a baked cake
      never turns back into its mixture.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.what-melts k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.what-melts count=2 seed=2
    practice k.heat-or-cold count=2 seed=3
    show k.ice-gone k=0 hard-k=1
    practice k.set-again count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.sugar-in-water
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.egg-cannot-go-back c="cook-egg" hard-c="bake-cake"
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.sun-or-shade
  }
  remember {
    say "Warm it and it melts. Cool it and it sets. Some changes cannot go back."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Melting is best watched: an ice cube on a saucer on the table and
    another on a sunny windowsill, looked at every ten minutes. Keep hot water and the hob for the
    grown-up. If the child thinks the sugar has vanished, let them taste the water. Off the page,
    make chocolate crispy cakes and talk about the chocolate melting and setting again. Japan,
    幼稚園教育要領 (kindergarten guidelines), 環境, 内容 (2): "生活の中で，様々な物に触れ，その性質や仕組みに興味や関心をもつ。"
    (Touch many things in daily life and take an interest in how they behave and work.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the ice lolly is chosen in the teacher's pen first, the changes are melting ice and chocolate, the ice melts in the sun, and the two-star is the egg."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the things to melt are chocolate and butter, the changes include freezing and toast, the ice melts on the table, and the two-star is the cake."
  }
}
